Algorithm:

Step 1: Create a boolean array dp of size sum + 1 to store whether a particular sum is possible.
Step 2: Initialize dp[0] as true because a sum of 0 is always possible with no elements.
Step 3: Traverse all elements of the given array one by one.
Step 4: For each element, traverse the dp array from sum down to the value of the current element.
Step 5: For every position, check whether the current sum can be formed either by excluding the element or including it.
Step 6: Update the dp array accordingly to mark that sum as possible.
Step 7: Continue this process for all elements in the array.
Step 8: After processing all elements, check the value stored at dp[sum].
Step 9: If dp[sum] is true, it means a subset exists with the required sum.
Step 10: Return the value stored at dp[sum].

Time Complexity: O(n × sum)
Space Complexity: O(sum)


-----> Code <-----

class Solution {
    static Boolean isSubsetSum(int arr[], int sum) {
        // code here
        boolean[] dp=new boolean[sum+1];
        dp[0]=true;
        for(int i=0;i<arr.length;i++){
            for(int j=sum;j>=arr[i];j--){
                dp[j]=(dp[j] || dp[j-arr[i]]);
            }
        }
        return dp[sum];
    }
}
